now that the war with Italy was over the  one against the Saxons which had been  interrupted for the time being was taken  up once more no war ever undertaken by  the Frankish people was more prolonged  more full of atrocities or more  demanding of effort the Saxons  like almost all the people living in  Germany are ferocious by nature they are  much given to devil worship and they are  hostile to our religion they think it no  dishonor to violate and transgress the  laws of God and man hardly a day passed  without some incident or other which was  well calculated to break the peace our  brothers and theirs were contiguous and  nearly everywhere in flat open country  except indeed for a few places where  great forests or mountain ranges  interposed to separate the territories  of the two people by a clear demarcation  line murder robbery and arson were of  constant occurrence on both sides in the  end the Franks was so irritated by these  incidents that they decided that the  time had come to abandon retaliatory  measures and to undertake a full-scale  war against the Saxons  war was duly declared against them  it was waged for 33 long years and with  immense hatred on both sides but the  losses of the Saxons were greater than  those of the Franks this war could have  been brought to a more rapid conclusion  had it not been for the faithlessness of  the Saxons it was hard to say just how  many times they were beaten and  surrended as suppliants to Charlemagne  promising to do all the was exalted from  them giving the hostages who were  demanded and this without delay and  receiving the ambassador's who were sent  to them sometimes they were so cowed and  reduced that they even promised to  abandon their devil-worship that submit  willingly to the Christian faith but  however ready they might seem from time  to time to do all this they were always  prepared to break the promises they had  made I cannot really judge which of  these two courses can be said to have  come the more easily to the Saxons for  since the very beginning of the war  against them hardly a year passed in  which they did not vacillate between  surrender and defiance  however the Kings mettlesome spirit and  his imperturbable 'ti which remained as  constant in adversity as in prosperity  were not to be quelled by their  ever-changing tactics or indeed to be  wearied by a task which he had  undertaken not once did he allow anyone  who had offended in this way to go  unpunished  he took vengeance on them for their  perfect and meted out suitable  punishment either by means of an army  which he led himself or by dispatching  of force against them under the command  of his counts in the end when all those  who'd been offering resistance had been  utterly defeated and subjected to his  power he transported some 10,000 men  taken from among those who lived on both  side of the river elbe and across the  river and dispersed them in small groups  with their wives and children in various  parts of Gaul and Germany at long last  this war which it dragged on for so many  years came to an end on conditions  imposed by the king and accepted by the  Saxons  these last were to give up their devil  worship and the malpractice is inherited  from their forefathers and then once  they had adopted the sacraments of the  Christian faith or religion they were to  be united with the Franks and become one  people with them  [Music]  [Music]
 

